## Encode ns/row (instrumented images)

The extension was built twice more with a temporary `clock_gettime` around the
encode call and an `elog(LOG, "FPQENC ...")` line (not committed). Both images
log per batch; `bench/internals`'s `__rdtsc` would have required a rebuild of the
client decode as well, so the server-side timer is the narrower one.

- `FPQENC` in the base image wraps `fastpostgres_publish_columnar_shm()`
  (`columnar_build`): the encode alone.
- `FPQENC` in the change image wraps `fastpostgres_columnar_fetch()` (executor
  `PortalRunFetch` + receiver encode + publish). A second change build with
  `FPQ_SCAN_ONLY=1` runs the same executor with a receiver that counts rows but
  writes nothing; **change encode = total − scan**. The subtraction removes the
  executor and the publish, leaving slot deform + column write.
- Table: the benchmark shape, 100 000-row batches, 20 pulls at 100K and 30
  pulls at 1M per image, warm pull first. Two runs per image.

| | merge/transport | ws/db-encode |
|---|---|---|
| encode-only, 100K | **34.1 / 34.1** ns/row | 46.7 / 46.7 ns/row |
| encode-only, 1M | **34.8 / 33.8** ns/row | 43.7 / 43.0 ns/row |
| whole NEXT (executor+encode), 100K | 102.8 / 102.6 | **68.3 / 66.5** |
| whole NEXT (executor+encode), 1M | 101.0 / 99.8 | **70.0 / 68.1** |

The one-pass row-major write is **~26–37% more expensive per encoded row** than
the old column-major loop: it writes six column regions per row (six cache
lines) where `columnar_build` swept one column at a time. It still wins end to
end: the receiver removes the `SPI_cursor_fetch` tuplestore materialisation,
which cost more than that locality difference. Whole worker NEXT drops
**−33.6% / −30.7%**; `stream()` at the client drops even more because the
DB-API/zero-copy path no longer receives a tuplestore-sized frame per chunk.
This is H8's stated trade: fewer copies, not a cheaper transpose.

## Frame identity

At `ec56e59` (H8-B, wire **v2**, before the offset table) the streamed receiver
was compared byte-for-byte with `fpq-baseline` for three shapes — bench, mixed
(NULLs, 1–54 B and 130–200 B varlena values, a non-ASCII value) and binary
(date/time/timestamp/timestamptz/uuid/bytea/numeric) — at n=1000 and n=100000,
with one full batch and with deliberate partial batches (777 and 33333). All 12
payload SHA-256 checksums and all payload sizes matched exactly, including the
terminal 0-row frame. Example (n=1000, batch=777):

```
bench   baseline sha=02faef3b… sizes=[33969, 9943]   change sha=02faef3b… sizes=[33969, 9943]
mixed   baseline sha=7da950f4… sizes=[92965, 27127]  change sha=7da950f4… sizes=[92965, 27127]
binary  baseline sha=3caca226… sizes=[50712, 14712]  change sha=3caca226… sizes=[50712, 14712]
```

At v3 the frame header/table necessarily changes (version 3, +8 B frame and
+8 B per column; codec generator payload 43 728 → 43 784 at 1000×6). The column
contents were checked instead: names, kinds, pgtypes, null bitmaps and data
regions hashed per column are identical to the main baseline for bench, mixed
and binary at n=1000/100000, full and partial batches (12/12 checksums match,
e.g. bench n=1000 batch=777 `4fc4349b…`, mixed `f5fdb63c…`, binary
`85ecc6e6…`).

## Tests

`docker run --rm --shm-size=3g fpq-db-encode tests.sh`:

```
### extension: make installcheck
1..1
# All 1 tests passed.

### client: unittest
Ran 61 tests in 1.4s
OK (skipped=1)
```

The skipped test is the eventfd pool test, opt-in and unrelated. The new
`test_the_columnar_batch_ends_at_the_segment_and_carries` covers a 256 KiB
segment cutting a columnar batch and the carried row(s) resuming with no gap or
repeat.

## What remains

- **Cache locality of the one-pass write.** The encode-only regression is the
  row-major sweep. A small row-major tile (e.g. 1–4k rows) written column-major
  would recover it, but that is H4's scratch again, just smaller; it is not in
  this change.
- **Wire v3 is not backward compatible.** A v3 reader refuses a v2 frame and a
  v2 reader refuses v3 (both check `FASTPOSTGRES_COLUMNAR_VERSION`). The client
  and extension are built from the same header in this repository/image, which
  is the deployment model; an independently upgraded client would need a
  version-negotiation path.
- **Gaps stay in the payload.** A full batch publishes the reservation's header
  positions, so `payload_size` includes unwritten gap bytes between a column's
  exact `data_len` and the next header. No reader touches them and the shm path
  does not copy the payload, but a transport that copied it would pay for them.
- **Compaction is still needed for the last/partial batch** (null bitmap and
  offset stride shrink). That is one batch per query, not every batch.
